---
// THE FIRST-VISIT GATE. Rendered from index.astro ONLY — never BaseLayout, so no other route can inherit a
// full-screen interstitial. See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-01-first-visit-gate-design.md.
//
// THE ONE INVARIANT THAT MATTERS: this ships NOT COVERING, and JS raises it. The static HTML carries the gate
// in its dismissed state and an inline script adds .is-up. A click-gate that defaulted to covering would lock
// out every visitor whose JS failed, and would also hide the page from anything that does not run scripts.
// The page's full markup is in the HTML either way, so this is an overlay, not a content gate — which is what
// keeps it crawlable. Same trick as BaseLayout's descent-veil, which parks itself off-screen by default.
// tests/distSmoke.test.ts asserts the shipped HTML carries no covering class, so the invariant is checked
// against the build rather than trusted.
import { name, roles } from '../data/profile';
// THE LINES ARE THE ORIGINAL 21st.dev GEOMETRY, ported. Six candidates were built as real full-screen gates in
// /proto-gate — descent trails, combed contours of the hero's own field, and four black-hole readings — and the
// owner's verdict was "the original is the best". It is a BORROWED drawing rather than one this site derived,
// which is a real cost and is written up in lib/gateRefPaths.ts; it should carry a credit if it stays.
//
// What makes it work, and what every original attempt here failed to reproduce: the family CONVERGES (the
// offset enters the two halves of each curve with opposite sign, so the left end moves right while the right
// end moves left) and its opacity ramps all the way 0.1 -> 1.0, so most strokes recede and a reader picks out
// about eight over a haze. Our combed version structurally could not do the first part — its no-knot guarantee
// needs a tilt that exceeds the field's maximum gradient, which forces the strokes parallel.
import { GATE_DISMISS_MS, GATE_REVEAL_EVENT, GATE_SEEN_KEY, GATE_UP_ATTR } from '../lib/gate';

// NO GEOMETRY IS RENDERED AT BUILD TIME ANY MORE, which is why nothing is imported from gateRefPaths here.
// The field is a canvas, and the module script at the bottom of this file imports the curves itself — so the
// 48 `d`-strings never reach the HTML at all. That is a few KB off every homepage response, and it means there
// is exactly one place the geometry is read from rather than one in the markup and one in the script.
const letters = [...`${name.first} ${name.last}`];
---

<div class="gate" data-gate role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="gate-name">
  {/* A CANVAS, NOT AN SVG, AND THE REASON IS THE TAIL.

      The brief: "what i imagine is lines travelling through the background like the trails of asteroids." An
      asteroid trail is a bright HEAD with a tail that decays smoothly to nothing — not a lit section of a line
      that is otherwise present. Six SVG dash schemes were built trying to get there and all six were rejected,
      because a dash can only ever make a line partial: its gap reads as the line vanishing, or, with anything
      drawn underneath, as a trail hanging off it. Both were reported in those words.

      A decaying tail is not expressible as a dash at all. It needs per-pixel alpha falloff along the stroke,
      which on a canvas is one line of compositing — each frame the whole surface is faded with
      `destination-out` and only the newest sliver of travel is drawn, so every head trails an exponential that
      goes to zero. In SVG the same effect would be a stack of sub-paths per curve with hand-computed opacities,
      rebuilt every frame.

      The CURVES ARE UNCHANGED: still the ported 21st.dev family, still smoothed at the join, still at the zoom
      measured against the owner's reference. They are no longer things that get drawn, though — they are the
      TRACKS the heads travel along. `lib/gateComets.ts` turns each one into an arc-length-parameterised
      polyline so travel is at constant speed, and `fitMeet` there reproduces `preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid
      meet"` by hand, because the renderer is no longer doing that arithmetic for us and the composition must
      not shift just because the element type did.

      This also makes the gate a relative of the hero instead of an unrelated borrowed drawing: `TerrainHero`
      paints its gradient-descent walkers as fading comet trails, which is the same language. */}
  <canvas class="gate-field" data-gate-field aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

  <div class="gate-text">
    {/* A <p>, NOT an <h1>, and that is a correctness fix rather than a style choice. The gate shipped with
        an <h1> first and tests/distSmoke.test.ts caught it immediately: "index.html has 2 <h1> elements; a
        document gets exactly one". The hero's name is the homepage's real heading and this is a loader on
        top of it, so the second <h1> was a duplicate outline, not a second section — and the a11y bar here
        is 100. The dialog keeps its accessible name because aria-labelledby may point at ANY element, so
        nothing about the announcement changes. */}
    <p class="gate-name" id="gate-name">
      {letters.map((ch, i) => (
        ch === ' '
          ? <span class="gate-space"> </span>
          : <span class="gate-letter" style={`--l:${i}`}>{ch}</span>
      ))}
    </p>
    <p class="gate-roles">{roles}</p>
    <button class="gate-enter" type="button" data-gate-enter>
      enter the descent <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* PARKED, NOT COVERING. Default state is fully dismissed: no JS means no gate. */
  .gate {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 10000; /* above .descent-veil's 9999 — if both ever raise, the gate owns the screen */
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background: var(--bg);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .gate.is-up {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .gate-field { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  /* THE STROKES ARE PAPER, NOT OCHRE, because the source draws them in `currentColor` under
     `dark:text-white` — white threads on a near-black ground. Ochre was my substitution and it is most of why
     the field read as muddy: the opacity ramp starts at 0.1, and a GOLD line at a tenth opacity over near-black
     has almost no luminance left to carry, where a near-white one still reads as light. `--paper` is the site's
     ivory rather than pure #fff, which keeps palette discipline without losing the original's effect. */
  /* The canvas reads its ink off this, via getComputedStyle, so the trails stay on a palette token instead of
     a hardcoded rgba — the same mistake the gate's button label made once (a frozen light-theme --paper that
     changed hue against a live var on hover). */
  .gate-field { color: var(--paper); }

  .gate-text { position: relative; text-align: center; padding: 0 7vw; }

  .gate-name {
    font-family: var(--font-display), Georgia, serif;
    color: var(--paper);
    font-size: clamp(44px, 9vw, 116px);
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .gate-letter, .gate-space { display: inline-block; }
  .gate-space { width: 0.3em; }

  .gate-roles {
    /* Display italic, matching Heights.astro's treatment of the same string — the dissolve only reads as
       one screen if the words land in the same voice as well as the same place. The spec said mono; the
       hero reserves mono for the line BELOW this one, so matching the hero wins. */
    font-family: var(--font-display), Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 62%, transparent);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 2.2vw, 21px);
    margin: 18px 0 0;
  }

  .gate-enter {
    /* The site's existing chip treatment (see sections/Signature.astro), not the reference's glassmorphic
       panel. There is no such vocabulary here and adding one for a single button is what the taste rules
       exist to prevent. */
    margin-top: 34px;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    padding: 10px 18px;
    border-radius: 9999px;
    background: transparent;
    /* A TOKEN, NOT A HARDCODED RGBA. This was rgba(239,233,221,0.8) — the LIGHT theme's --paper, frozen —
       while :hover below resolves var(--paper) live, so in dark theme the label changed hue on hover
       (#efe9dd -> #dfe3df). Signature.astro, the model this chip is borrowed from, uses text-paper/80,
       which follows the theme. Palette discipline is a taste rule here, not a preference. */
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 80%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ochre) 25%, transparent);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .gate-enter:hover { color: var(--paper); border-color: var(--ochre); }
  /* :focus AS WELL AS :focus-visible. The script focuses this button programmatically, and Chrome does not
     match :focus-visible on a programmatic focus with no prior keyboard interaction — so the only control
     in a modal dialog had no visible focus indicator until the visitor happened to press a key. */
  .gate-enter:focus,
  .gate-enter:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ochre); outline-offset: 3px; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .gate { transition: opacity 600ms ease, visibility 600ms ease; }

    /* THE MOTION IS A DRIFT OF THE WHOLE FIELD, NOT A DASH ON THE STROKES.
         The history is worth keeping short, because its conclusion is now settled: five dash schemes shipped
       here — a one-shot, a CSS translation of framer-motion's pathLength/pathOffset (four times, each failing
       differently: a sub-unit stipple, a calc() that never interpolated, a fixed dash sliding in and out of
       frame), then a faithful JS reproduction, then a seamless constant-length version over a static haze. The
       last two were correct implementations and still lost, because the problem is not the implementation. A
       dash is a partial line. Its gap is either empty (the line vanishes) or faintly filled (the line trails).
       Both were reported, and they are the same artifact.
         So the strokes carry no animation at all now, and the motion is two transforms on two groups — which is
       also what the TASTE RULES want: transform only, composited, no per-frame paint. An earlier version of this
       was judged "basically static", so the amplitude here is deliberately larger than that one's and the two
       families counter-drift rather than moving together, which is what makes the flow legible.
         DRIFT ALONG THE LINES' OWN DIRECTION. The second cubic's tangent is (464, 127) for every curve in both
       families — independent of the offset, so it really is the family's shared direction — which normalises to
       (0.964, 0.264). Translating along it slides each curve approximately along ITSELF, so the field reads as
       flowing rather than as a picture being shoved sideways. Translating along any other axis reads as the
       latter immediately.
         It alternates rather than looping. A one-way loop would need the translation to map the family onto
       itself, which is exactly one curve's offset — but the family is finite, so its first and last curve have
       no neighbour to become, and the opacity ramp would snap back at every wrap. Alternating has no wrap. */

    /* THE LETTERS FAIL VISIBLE, and that is the fix for the name arriving incomplete TWICE — "I g T n" and
       then "ng Ti", a different set of letters missing each time.
         What was wrong: the rule set `opacity: 0` as the resting state and relied on a `forwards` animation to
       bring each letter back. Any letter whose animation does not run, or gets interrupted and never restarts,
       is then permanently invisible — and the thing it takes with it is the owner's NAME, on the first screen
       of his portfolio. My earlier explanation for this (frame starvation) was wrong on its own terms: CSS
       animations are driven by the document timeline, so dropped frames make them jerky, never unfinished.
       Whatever the real trigger is, the failure mode was only possible because the resting state was invisible.
         So nothing here starts at opacity 0 any more. The spring animates TRANSFORM only, and a letter whose
       animation never runs simply sits in its final position, fully legible. The same asymmetry as isCovered()
       and hasSeenGate(): decide which direction the unknown case should fail in, and make that the default. */
    .gate.is-up .gate-letter {
      animation: gate-letter 520ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1) both;
      animation-delay: calc(150ms + var(--l) * 30ms);
    }
    @keyframes gate-letter {
      from { transform: translateY(36px); }
      to   { transform: none; }
    }

    /* THESE FAIL VISIBLE TOO. They had `opacity: 0` with a `forwards` fade, which is the same hazard the
       letters had — and it bit for the same reason: a screenshot arrived with the name present but the roles
       line AND the button both missing, on a modal whose only control is that button. Nothing on this screen
       may have an invisible resting state. They rise instead, from a state that is already legible. */
    .gate.is-up .gate-roles,
    .gate.is-up .gate-enter {
      animation: gate-rise 600ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1) both;
    }
    .gate.is-up .gate-roles { animation-delay: 400ms; }
    .gate.is-up .gate-enter { animation-delay: 700ms; }
    @keyframes gate-rise {
      from { transform: translateY(14px); }
      to   { transform: none; }
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* THE FINISHED STATE IS NOW GENUINELY THE SAME PICTURE, which is the first time that has been true here.
       Earlier versions had to undo something: a dash reset, because leaving stroke-dasharray set with the
       animation disabled renders every stroke partially drawn — a broken state wearing a finished state's
       clothes — and an opacity pin, because the group opacity was itself animated so the unanimated state was
       brighter than anything a moving reader saw. Neither applies: the strokes carry no animated property at
       all, and the only motion is a transform on the two groups. Parking that transform at the midpoint of its
       range leaves exactly the drawing everyone else sees, mid-drift. */
    /* Nothing to park: the canvas checks prefersReducedMotion() itself and paints ONE finished frame of
       trails mid-flight rather than animating them. A still of the same picture, which is what the motion rule
       asks for, and it has to be the canvas's own branch because a CSS rule cannot reach into a rAF loop. */
    .gate-letter, .gate-roles, .gate-enter { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }

  /* NO PHONE MEDIA QUERY HERE ANY MORE. It used to hide every other SVG path; the canvas halves its own comet
     count through isPhone() from lib/viewport.ts, which is the one phone gate.
       Note for the hand-sync checklist in CLAUDE.md that counts phone breakpoints by grep: this file has
     dropped out of it. The breakpoint is deliberately NOT written out in this comment — spelling it here would
     keep the file in the grep's results as a hit that is only prose, which is precisely the kind of false entry
     that made that checklist wrong before. */
</style>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ KEY: GATE_SEEN_KEY, MS: GATE_DISMISS_MS, UP: GATE_UP_ATTR, REVEAL: GATE_REVEAL_EVENT }}>
  // Synchronous, so a first visit covers before first paint with no flash of homepage-then-gate. Mirrors
  // BaseLayout's veil script, which is inline for the same reason.
  //
  // The policy this duplicates lives in lib/gate.ts and is unit-tested there. It is restated inline rather
  // than imported because a module script cannot run before first paint, and the whole value of the gate's
  // no-flash behaviour depends on running early. The two must agree: KEY and MS are injected from the same
  // constants, and lib/gate.ts's tests are what pin the semantics (fail closed, timer not transitionend,
  // idempotent). If you change the rule, change it there first.
  (function () {
    var gate = document.querySelector('[data-gate]');
    if (!gate) return;

    // FRESH LOAD ONLY. An inline script runs during parse, so readyState is 'loading'; when ClientRouter
    // re-executes it after a View Transition the document is already 'complete'. Without this the gate
    // raised mid-session: the script lives only in /'s HTML, so a visitor whose entry page was /research
    // (the indexed, linkable entry) is not carrying it in Astro's `scriptsAlreadyRan`, and clicking "home"
    // executed it for the first time — a full-screen interstitial over a page they were already going to.
    // BaseLayout's veil has guarded the identical hazard since it was written (`__descentVeilInit`,
    // "first load only"). isFreshLoad() in lib/gate.ts owns this rule and is tested there.
    if (document.readyState !== 'loading') return;

    var store = null;
    try { store = window.sessionStorage; } catch (e) { store = null; }

    // FAIL CLOSED: no store, or a store that throws, means do not raise. See lib/gate.ts.
    var seen = true;
    if (store) {
      try { seen = store.getItem(KEY) !== null; } catch (e) { seen = true; }
    }
    if (seen) return;

    var done = false;
    var btn = gate.querySelector('[data-gate-enter]');

    function reduced() {
      return window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    }

    // STOPIMMEDIATEPROPAGATION, NOT JUST PREVENTDEFAULT — and the difference is the whole bug this fixes.
    // preventDefault() cancels the browser's own scrolling; it does nothing to a SIBLING listener. Deck.astro
    // also listens on window for wheel, and its onWheel does NOT check e.defaultPrevented (it checks it for
    // keydown, at Deck.astro:243, but not for wheel). So one flick while the gate was up ran the deck's
    // tween — window.scrollTo() still works under `overflow: hidden`, which by spec suppresses only the
    // USER's scrolling mechanism and leaves programmatic scrolling intact — and the visitor clicked "enter
    // the descent" to arrive in the middle of a later slide, never seeing the hero the gate exists to
    // introduce. This listener is registered at parse time and the deck's at astro:page-load, so the gate's
    // runs first and stopImmediatePropagation keeps the deck from ever seeing the event.
    function swallow(e) {
      if (done) return;
      e.preventDefault();
      e.stopImmediatePropagation();
    }

    // The deck's keys have to be swallowed for the same reason, and the previous version let every one of
    // them through: it handled Escape and Tab and returned, so ArrowDown/PageDown/space/Home/End reached
    // Deck.onKey and paged the document behind the gate.
    var DECK_KEYS = [
      'ArrowDown', 'ArrowUp', 'PageDown', 'PageUp', 'Home', 'End', ' ', 'Spacebar',
    ];

    function onKey(e) {
      if (e.key === 'Escape') { dismiss(); return; }
      if (e.key === 'Tab') {
        // A REAL TRAP, NOT A FREEZE. Blanket-blocking Tab looked like a trap but was one: clicking the
        // gate's background blurs the button to <body>, and from there a blocked Tab left a keyboard
        // visitor unable to reach the only control, with Escape the undocumented sole exit. Put focus back
        // on the button instead, so Tab cycles where there is one stop.
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopImmediatePropagation();
        if (btn) btn.focus();
        return;
      }
      if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === 'Spacebar' || e.key === ' ') {
        // Let the button's own activation through, but never the deck's page-down on space.
        if (e.target === btn) return;
      }
      if (DECK_KEYS.indexOf(e.key) !== -1) {
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopImmediatePropagation();
      }
    }

    // EVERY BODY CHILD EXCEPT THE GATE, not just <main>. `inert` on main alone left 18 focusable elements
    // reachable — measured against dist: 7 Toc links, 5 CornerNav page links, 4 CornerNav mark links
    // (GitHub / mailto / cv.pdf / LinkedIn), the nav menu button and the theme toggle, all of which render
    // AFTER </main>. The old comment claimed "the only focusable thing is the button", which was true of
    // the gate and false of the document, so the trap rested entirely on blanket-blocking Tab.
    var inerted = [];
    function setInert(on) {
      if (on) {
        var kids = document.body.children;
        for (var i = 0; i < kids.length; i++) {
          var k = kids[i];
          if (k === gate || k.tagName === 'SCRIPT' || k.hasAttribute('inert')) continue;
          k.setAttribute('inert', '');
          inerted.push(k);
        }
      } else {
        for (var j = 0; j < inerted.length; j++) inerted[j].removeAttribute('inert');
        inerted = [];
      }
    }

    function dismiss() {
      if (done) return;            // idempotent: Escape then click must not run this twice
      done = true;
      try { if (store) store.setItem(KEY, '1'); } catch (e) { /* denied; it reappears next load */ }

      gate.classList.remove('is-up');
      document.documentElement.style.overflow = '';
      // Let the hero and the sky start animating: they held off while nothing they drew could be seen.
      // Order matters — clear the flag BEFORE firing, so a listener that re-reads it sees the new state.
      document.documentElement.removeAttribute(UP);
      document.dispatchEvent(new Event(REVEAL));
      setInert(false);
      window.removeEventListener('wheel', swallow, { passive: false });
      window.removeEventListener('touchmove', swallow, { passive: false });
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey, true);
      document.removeEventListener('astro:before-swap', dismiss);

      // A TIMER, never transitionend — under reduced motion no transition fires and this would never run,
      // leaving the page scroll-locked forever. dismissMs() in lib/gate.ts owns this number.
      window.setTimeout(function () {
        // The deck measured its stops while the document was scroll-locked; make it re-measure.
        window.dispatchEvent(new Event('resize'));
      }, reduced() ? 0 : MS);
    }

    // THIS SCRIPT DOES NOT DRAW ANYTHING. It owns the gate's policy — raise, swallow, dismiss, teardown — and
    // has to be inline to run before first paint. The field is painted by the module script at the bottom of
    // this file, which can import from lib/ precisely because it is NOT inline and does not need to beat the
    // first frame. Six dash schemes were driven from here before this; the reason none stayed is in the markup
    // comment above the canvas.

    gate.classList.add('is-up');
    document.documentElement.style.overflow = 'hidden';
    // Raised BEFORE the canvases initialise, because this script is inline and parse-time while theirs run on
    // astro:page-load — so they see the flag on their very first check and never start a loop at all, rather
    // than starting one and being stopped a moment later.
    document.documentElement.setAttribute(UP, '');
    setInert(true);
    window.addEventListener('wheel', swallow, { passive: false });
    window.addEventListener('touchmove', swallow, { passive: false });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey, true);

    // LEAVING THE PAGE IS A DISMISSAL, and without this the listeners outlived the element they belong to.
    // ClientRouter replaces document.body on a navigation, so the gate's DOM disappears while `swallow` and
    // `onKey` — bound to window and document, which survive the swap — keep calling preventDefault forever:
    // wheel and trackpad scrolling dead on every page, Tab dead site-wide, for the rest of the session, with
    // no gate on screen to explain it. The visitor concludes their mouse broke. CLAUDE.md's Interactivity
    // contract has required "re-init on astro:page-load, WITH A TEARDOWN" all along; this is the teardown.
    document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', dismiss);

    if (btn) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', dismiss);
      try { btn.focus({ preventScroll: true }); } catch (e) { btn.focus(); }
    }
  })();
</script>

<script>
  // ── THE ASTEROID TRAILS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // A bundled module script, NOT inline, which is what lets it import the geometry and the two gates instead of
  // restating them. It is allowed to be late: the inline script above has already raised the gate before first
  // paint, so this only has to arrive before a visitor has finished reading a name.
  //
  // Everything pure is in lib/gateComets.ts with a spec; what stays here is the plumbing the Interactivity
  // contract says stays here — the canvas, the rAF loop, resize, visibility, and teardown.
  import { GATE_COUNT, REF_POSITIONS, refFamily, refViewBox } from '../lib/gateRefPaths';
  import { fitMeet, jitter, parseTrack, pointAt, type Track } from '../lib/gateComets';
  import { prefersReducedMotion } from '../lib/motion';
  import { isPhone } from '../lib/viewport';

  interface Comet { track: Track; s: number; speed: number; alpha: number; width: number }

  // HOW LONG A TAIL LIVES, in seconds rather than in frames. The fade is applied per frame, so a per-frame
  // constant would make tail length depend on the frame rate — shorter on a 120Hz display, longer when the
  // machine is busy. Expressed as a time constant and converted with the real dt, the streak is the same length
  // everywhere. 0.62s of decay at the speed below is a tail of roughly 250 CSS px.
  const TAIL_TAU = 0.62;
  // Arc units per second. The visible window is about a quarter of each ~1580-unit curve, so ~70 puts a head
  // across the frame in about six seconds: fast enough to read as travelling, slow enough not to strobe.
  const SPEED = 70;
  const SPEED_SPREAD = 0.45;   // +/- 45%, so heads never move as a block
  const DPR_CAP = 2;           // the hero's cap, for the same reason: 3x on a phone buys nothing visible here

  let raf = 0;
  let stop: (() => void) | null = null;

  function start(): void {
    const maybeCanvas = document.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('[data-gate-field]');
    const maybeGate = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-gate]');
    if (!maybeCanvas || !maybeGate) return;
    // Non-null aliases straight after the guard: TypeScript does not carry a const's narrowing into the hoisted
    // functions below, and that is exactly the ts(18047) class CLAUDE.md warns will come back.
    const cv = maybeCanvas;
    const gate = maybeGate;
    const maybeCtx = cv.getContext('2d');
    if (!maybeCtx) return;
    const c2 = maybeCtx;

    // If the gate is not covering there is nothing to draw over and no reason to hold a loop — a repeat visit in
    // the same session never raises it.
    if (!gate.classList.contains('is-up')) return;

    const vb = refViewBox().split(/\s+/).map(Number) as [number, number, number, number];
    const ink = getComputedStyle(cv).color || 'rgb(239,233,221)';
    const reduced = prefersReducedMotion();

    // One comet per curve; on a phone, every other one. The content is identical either way, which is this
    // project's phone rule, but a full-screen canvas redrawing 48 streaks is a real cost on a handset.
    const step = isPhone() ? 2 : 1;
    const comets: Comet[] = [];
    let n = 0;
    for (const p of REF_POSITIONS) {
      const fam = refFamily(p, GATE_COUNT, true);
      for (let i = 0; i < fam.length; i += step) {
        const t = fam[i];
        const track = parseTrack(t.d);
        comets.push({
          track,
          // Phases spread over the WHOLE track, not just its visible part, so at any moment some heads are
          // mid-frame, some are still off the top-left, and some have already left past the bottom-right. That
          // is what makes them read as independent objects rather than as a formation — the aligned-front
          // defect, which cost a round of its own, is impossible here for the same reason.
          s: jitter(n, 1) * track.total,
          speed: SPEED * (1 + (jitter(n, 7) - 0.5) * 2 * SPEED_SPREAD),
          alpha: t.opacity,
          width: t.width,
        });
        n++;
      }
    }

    let fit = { scale: 1, dx: 0, dy: 0 };
    let cssW = 0;
    let cssH = 0;

    /** Project a point from the curves' user space into CSS px. */
    function px(q: { x: number; y: number }): { x: number; y: number } {
      return { x: q.x * fit.scale + fit.dx, y: q.y * fit.scale + fit.dy };
    }

    /** Stroke between two arc-length positions as a polyline, so it follows the curve instead of cutting it. */
    function arc(cm: Comet, from: number, to: number, alpha: number): void {
      const span = to - from;
      if (span <= 0 || alpha <= 0.002) return;
      const steps = Math.max(1, Math.min(8, Math.ceil(span / 6)));
      c2.beginPath();
      for (let k = 0; k <= steps; k++) {
        const q = px(pointAt(cm.track, from + (span * k) / steps));
        if (k === 0) c2.moveTo(q.x, q.y);
        else c2.lineTo(q.x, q.y);
      }
      c2.globalAlpha = alpha;
      c2.lineWidth = Math.max(0.6, cm.width * fit.scale);
      c2.strokeStyle = ink;
      c2.stroke();
    }

    /**
     * THE FINISHED STATE for a reader with motion off: the same picture, held still. The falloff is drawn
     * explicitly here because the animated version gets its taper from repeated compositing, and a still frame
     * has no sequence of frames to accumulate one from.
     */
    function still(): void {
      c2.clearRect(0, 0, cssW, cssH);
      const SEGS = 26;
      for (const cm of comets) {
        const tail = cm.speed * TAIL_TAU * 2.2;
        for (let k = 0; k < SEGS; k++) {
          arc(cm, cm.s - (tail * (k + 1)) / SEGS, cm.s - (tail * k) / SEGS, cm.alpha * Math.exp((-k / SEGS) * 2.2));
        }
      }
      c2.globalAlpha = 1;
    }

    function resize(): void {
      const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, DPR_CAP);
      cssW = cv.clientWidth;
      cssH = cv.clientHeight;
      cv.width = Math.max(1, Math.round(cssW * dpr));
      cv.height = Math.max(1, Math.round(cssH * dpr));
      c2.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);   // draw in CSS px; the transform handles density
      c2.clearRect(0, 0, cssW, cssH);
      fit = fitMeet(vb, cssW, cssH);
      c2.lineCap = 'round';
      if (reduced) still();
    }

    let last = 0;
    function frame(now: number): void {
      // Clamp dt. A backgrounded tab or one long task hands back a gap of seconds, and advancing the heads by
      // that would teleport them while the fade wiped every tail in a single step.
      const dt = last ? Math.min((now - last) / 1000, 0.05) : 0.016;
      last = now;

      // THE TAIL, IN ONE OPERATION. destination-out scales down the alpha of what is already on the canvas, so
      // every previously drawn sliver decays toward transparent at a fixed time constant while the canvas itself
      // stays transparent over the gate behind it. Fading toward a background COLOUR instead would require an
      // opaque canvas, and an opaque full-screen layer is what the fluid sky's first invariant exists to avoid.
      c2.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out';
      c2.globalAlpha = 1 - Math.exp(-dt / TAIL_TAU);
      c2.fillStyle = '#000';
      c2.fillRect(0, 0, cssW, cssH);
      c2.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';

      for (const cm of comets) {
        const from = cm.s;
        cm.s += cm.speed * dt;
        if (cm.s >= cm.track.total) {
          // Wrap. Both ends of every curve sit well outside the viewBox — the family runs from (-380,-189) to
          // (684,875) inside a box of (-124,-56,944,429) — so a head restarts off-screen and no reset is ever
          // visible. That is the seam every dash scheme here fought with, solved by geometry instead of by
          // arithmetic.
          cm.s = 0;
          continue;
        }
        arc(cm, from, cm.s, cm.alpha);
      }
      c2.globalAlpha = 1;
      raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
    }

    function onVis(): void {
      if (document.hidden) {
        if (raf) { cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; }
      } else if (!raf && !reduced) {
        last = 0;
        raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
      }
    }

    resize();
    window.addEventListener('resize', resize);
    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', onVis);
    if (!reduced) raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);

    stop = () => {
      if (raf) { cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; }
      window.removeEventListener('resize', resize);
      document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', onVis);
      stop = null;
    };
  }

  // Teardown on every exit. ClientRouter replaces document.body, so without this the resize and visibility
  // listeners outlive the canvas they draw on — trap 4 in CLAUDE.md, one layer down. Dismissal fires the reveal
  // event, after which there is nothing left to draw over.
  document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', () => stop?.());
  window.addEventListener('descent:revealed', () => stop?.());
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => { stop?.(); start(); });
  if (document.readyState !== 'loading') start();
  else document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', start, { once: true });
</script>
